| 2026-08-02 | FIX-P0-REG | **Registration could not be submitted — root cause found, fixed, and the two reasons tests missed it closed.** **Exact root cause:** `POST /registrations` answered **`503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE`** because the `SystemSetting` row `legal.consent_text_version` did not exist. Registration **fails closed by design** — §4.1a requires the exact consent text version on every `ConsentRecord`, and recording that somebody agreed to text nobody approved would be worse than refusing — so the *refusal* was correct. **Three things were not.** **(1) The row had no way to exist.** §15.1's normative seed list does not include it (§2.3 makes legally versioning the Arabic text an owner compliance task), and the value is meant to be entered through `/superadmin/settings` (§5.6) — **a screen no milestone has built.** There was therefore no path anywhere in the product to set a value the flow requires. Now seeded by the **development fixtures** (§15.2, `NODE_ENV`-guarded, value literally `dev-unapproved-v1`), which makes the flow testable without inventing a legally-approved version for production. **(2) The error was unactionable.** The envelope carried an empty `details`, so a permanent configuration gap was indistinguishable from a transient outage and the form said *"try again later"* — advice that could never work. TD-3.8 defines `details` as structured context for codes that carry it; it now reports `reason: CONSENT_TEXT_VERSION_NOT_CONFIGURED` and names the setting. **(3) The test suites were deleting it.** Every registration-touching suite upserted the key in `beforeEach` and **deleted it in `afterAll`** — so running `npm run test:integration` actively broke the developer's environment. Green tests, broken application, tests the cause. Replaced with a shared `captureConsentVersion`/`restoreConsentVersion` helper that restores the prior value, **captured once** (a `beforeEach` capture would re-save the previous test's scratch value). **Why tests did not catch it:** a service test *did* assert `SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE` and passed throughout — it pinned the status and never that the failure was actionable — and **there was no HTTP-level test for registration at all**, so nothing tested what a browser receives. Both closed: the service test now asserts `details`, and a new `registration.http.integration.test.ts` (**11 tests**) covers the 503 envelope, a successful 201, the R40 composition over the wire, and the boundary refusals. **Error handling generalised:** `ApiError` now carries the TD-3.8 envelope (`code`, `details`, `request_id`) instead of the status alone — it could not interpret a body it was never given — and `explainFailure` branches on the server's own code, falling back to a generic message only when the cause is genuinely unknown. The request id is shown discreetly beside the error (§14.4). **Verified live:** the previously failing submission now returns **201**, and the row shows `خديجة \| بنعلي \| خديجة بنعلي \| pending`. The setting survives a full 520-test run | Registration is usable end to end in development | None |
