| 2026-08-02 | SRS-R42 | **Platform Settings — the production mechanism, replacing the deployment note.** `legal.consent_text_version` had **no way to be set in production**: §15.1 does not seed it (§2.3 owner task) and `/superadmin/settings` had no API in TD-3, so a first deployment refused every registration with no in-product remedy. New **TD-3.11**: `GET /admin/settings` and `PUT /admin/settings/{key}`, **Super Admin only** (asserted against live rows per TD-12 — verified live, where a *suspended* Super Admin was correctly refused). **An explicit writable allow-list, not "any key"** — `SystemSetting` also holds grading scales and category visibilities; an unlisted key is `NOT_FOUND`, never `403`, so a typo creates nothing and cannot confirm a key exists elsewhere (§20 rule 17). **Never empty:** blank, whitespace-only, non-string and >100 chars are refused, because a blank version would *look* configured while reproducing the very failure the setting prevents. **Audited as `setting.update` with OLD and NEW value** (TD-8, new row) — a record carrying only the new value cannot answer *"what text was in force when this person consented"*. **TD-15 optimistic locking**, with `create` setting `version: 1` so "never configured" (0) stays distinguishable from "configured once" — otherwise a client holding the 0 it read *before* the create would pass the check and silently overwrite. **Stored consents are never restamped** (normative): registration copies the version at the moment of agreement, and §4.1a is precisely what forbids rewriting it. Screen at `/superadmin/settings` renders what the server lists, with labels as server-chosen i18n keys so a client cannot drift from the allow-list; an unset value is called out as *"غير مضبوط — التسجيل متوقف"*. **11 backend tests** covering the permission boundary, the allow-list, every empty-value form, the audit pair, the stale write, and that a settings change leaves an existing `ConsentRecord` on its original version. **The development fixture is no longer the mechanism** — it remains a local convenience only | Settings module un-blocked in the registry; `settingDto` added (the DTO guard caught the new controller and was satisfied properly, not weakened) | None |
| 2026-08-02 | FIX-STAB | **Usability and workflow stabilisation — five defects, each root-caused before being touched.** **(1) Registration pages were dead ends.** The form, the success screen and the no-token screen carried no navigation at all: an applicant who had just submitted could not reach the site. All three now render the shared `ApplicationHeader` and `SiteFooter` — reused, not re-invented — plus explicit العودة إلى الرئيسية / تسجيل الدخول links and a line explaining that signing in shows the request's status (§2.1). **(2) `RATE_LIMITED` during normal testing — a classification defect, not a limit that was too low.** `/auth/refresh` sat in the **login** zone (10 r/m, burst 5) while the SPA calls it on **every fresh page load**, so **six page loads** exhausted it — measured: six succeeded, the seventh was refused. `/auth/refresh` and `/auth/logout` are now classified under the general-API limit **TD-13 already states** (120 r/m); the OAuth entry and callback keep 10 r/m. **No TD-13 number was changed** — the tighter limit protects credential *guessing*, and neither endpoint can be guessed: refresh presents a cookie the server issued and rotates, with TD-12 reuse detection revoking the whole session on replay. Verified after: refresh allows 21, `/auth/google` still refuses after 6. **Ruled out, each checked:** React StrictMode double-render (the single-flight promise collapses it to one network call), duplicate submissions (the button disables in flight), IP grouping (nginx is the edge, so the address is the real client). **(3) Login redirected to `/dashboard`, which is Not Found.** The callback **hardcoded** it. §4.1b step 4a calls this a *"role-based dashboard redirect"* and §14.1 defines the destinations — there is no bare `/dashboard` node. One authoritative policy now lives in `backend/src/lib/role-home.ts`: Super Admin and Admin → `/admin`, teacher → `/teacher`, parent/student → their §14.1 homes, and an account with **no** role → `/`, never a 404. **The header button had already been fixed; the server redirect had not** — the same defect surviving in a second place, now pinned by 6 tests including one asserting the client mirror agrees. **(4) The admin menu promised what the router did not deliver.** الحلقات, المستخدمون and الجدول والأنشطة carried `status: 'ready'` while no screen existed, so the sidebar showed them available and the page said *"هذا القسم قيد الإعداد"* — two answers to one question. They are now `blocked` with **named** reasons, the blocked state is decided **once** in `AdminRouter` (so sidebar, bookmark and direct link behave identically), and a new test asserts **every `ready` module has a screen and every screen is `ready`** — proven by restoring the inconsistency, which fails it. **(5) The Hijri calendar page was unusable from the day it was written.** Two defects in one file: `fetchHijriYear` was the **only** function in its adapter taking no token, so every read answered `401` and the screen rendered its error state; and `load`'s `useCallback` omitted `accessToken`, so the first attempt — made while the session was still resolving — was never retried. Passing the token was necessary but not sufficient. **Verified live end to end:** read year → record a month as draft → publish, all `200`. **The month recorded during that check was deleted immediately** — the date was invented for the test, and §20 rule 14 / Revision 32 forbid fabricated Hijri data existing anywhere | Registry and router kept in step by test; one `role-home` policy per side, both deriving from §14.1 | None |
| 2026-08-04 | SRS-R43 | **The educational model separates organisation from delivery — the largest structural revision since the initial blueprint.** **Why:** a `Group` was four facts in one row — a roster, a weekly timetable slot, a room booking, and a teacher assignment. That conflation is why a student could sit in only one slot per Level, why a Level could not teach two Subjects at different times to differently-split students, and why Revision 39 needed a whole revision to explain that `intended_branch_id` is a *request* rather than a placement. The association's actual model — one Level, several Subjects, each split differently, **Tafsir taught to everyone at once while Quran runs in three parallel groups** — **could not be expressed at all**. **New shape:** Category → Level → **AdministrativeGroup** → **Enrollment**, with **TeachingGroup** as an optional per-Subject split, **RecurringCourseSchedule** as the unit of delivery in three teaching modes (entire level / administrative group / teaching group), and **Session** as the materialized dated occurrence that owns notes, recordings, linked content and — later — attendance. **Eight decisions and the reasoning that has to survive.** **(1) Branch stays on the Administrative Group.** Delivery moves away from it; *place* does not. Without it "which branch is this person at" becomes many-valued (derived from wherever their sessions happen to be), R39's request-vs-placement distinction collapses, and staff branch scoping plus the §4.9 Global-content rule lose their subject. **(2) No `StudentLevel` — the proposal drafted under this number is WITHDRAWN.** Level membership is `Enrollment → AdministrativeGroup → Level`; a second table would drift. The "exactly one group per Level" rule spans two hops, so `Enrollment` carries `level_id` **plus a composite FK** `(administrative_group_id, level_id) → AdministrativeGroup(id, level_id)` — the database *refuses* a disagreeing row, which turns the redundant column into a **constraint rather than a copy** and is the only thing that permits a real `UNIQUE (student_id, level_id)` without a trigger. **(3) Teaching mode lives on the schedule and nowhere else** — the same Level teaches one Subject whole and another split, so it cannot be a property of Subject or Level. **A schedule targets exactly one entity**; plural targeting was rejected because it buys nothing a second row does not while forcing the roster query, the conflict check and the consent gate each to take a set. **(4) Sessions are materialized EAGERLY, and the reason is conflict detection.** Comparing recurrence *rules* cannot see that a weekly and a biweekly-alternating Tuesday 15:00 collide only on alternate weeks; comparing materialized rows can. Room, teacher **and assistant** overlap are all checked. **(5) A session carries its own date, time, room and teacher**, so a cancellation, a room change, a reschedule and a makeup class are **edits to a session row** — which collapses four Event cases into the Session model and narrows `Event` to genuinely non-teaching activity. A schedule edit **never touches** an overridden session or one carrying work, and reports what it skipped. **(6) Teacher scope moves from `GroupTeacher` to `CourseScheduleStaff`** — the single most load-bearing authorization derivation in the platform, feeding TD-2, Hidden-event visibility, sensitive social-data access, Quran logging and exam authoring. It also **generalises the two-slot co-teaching rule instead of duplicating it** and gives assistants a home without inventing a Role. Strictly better, too: `schedule.branch_id` states a teacher's branch directly where the old path inferred it through two hops. **Stated once in §4.4c; every other section points at it.** **(7) Quran becomes a Subject — for SCHEDULING ONLY.** §4.4b's exclusion was about *tracking* and stays true of tracking (memorization is Surah + ayah through the §4.5 engine, BR-13), but a timetable had no way to say *"this session is Quran"*. **A Quran `LevelSubject` generates NO grading components** (BR-9 carve-out) — the Adult-stage ones already come from `LevelSurah`, and both would give one body of work two competing sets. **(8) Room capacity informs and never refuses** (new BR-23). This deletes `Group.max_students`, its CHECK, the `CAPACITY_FULL` code, and the only roster-side `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` in TD-15.2 — **safe only because the invariant the lock guarded was removed with it**, which is recorded so a future capacity rule brings the lock back. **Specified but deliberately NOT built:** **attendance** (§4.7, per Session per student against the resolved audience) and **session announcements**. §20 rule 16 gains an explicit line — *a specification written ahead of its build is not permission to build it* — because §4.7 exists so the Session entity has the right shape, not so someone creates its table early. **New business rules:** BR-21 (one organisational group per level, and it holds the branch), BR-22 (splits optional; an unplaced student is never silent), BR-23 (capacity informs). **BR-2 restated on the session's resolved audience** — the former per-Group wording had no referent once a session could be for a teaching group or an entire level; content referenced by several sessions is gated by the **union**. **BR-17 rewritten** from "group-driven scheduling". **New §20 rules 22–24** pin the invariants an implementer would otherwise regress: never re-add room/teacher/schedule/capacity to an organisational group, never snapshot a session's audience, never drop the composite FK, never treat Quran as a tracked Subject, never let a schedule edit destroy work. **Scope:** re-opens M3 (which was complete and green) and inserts **M3b before M4**, since Quran progress and Exams both resolve students through the group model and building them against the old shape would mean writing them twice. Migration is **expand → migrate → contract across three deployments** (TD-6b): the `group` table is *renamed in vocabulary only*, and its schedule columns are dropped in a separate later migration | SRS §0/§4.1/§4.4/§4.4b/**§4.4c (new)**/§4.7/§4.9/§5/§7/§8/§9/§10.1/§12/§14.1/§15.1/§17/§18/§19.2/§20 and TD-1…TD-8, TD-15 revised; handbook, decision log, plan and TASKS updated in the same commit | None yet — M3b writes them |
| 2026-08-04 | SRS-R43.1 | **Document Owner adjustment — Administrative Group 1 is created WITH its Level, not lazily.** R43 had the seed create each Level's first group *lazily, on first Branch creation*, because §15.1 seeds Levels and forbids seeding Branches so no Branch exists at seed time. The Owner replaced that: **Level creation takes a required Branch and creates المجموعة 1 at it in the same transaction** (TD-4.6b), so the state *"Level with no group"* never occurs rather than existing until something fills it in. **The Branch is an INPUT to Level creation and is NOT a column on `Level`** — a Level stays Category-scoped and branch-independent (§4.4b) and may hold groups at several Branches; what the form asks is *where the first group sits*, and that answer is stored on the group, not the Level. **One bounded exception survives, and it is bootstrap rather than laziness:** seeded Levels necessarily predate every Branch, so **the first Branch created in a deployment backfills المجموعة 1 for every Level that has none** (TD-4.6d, atomic, idempotent, once per deployment by construction). Consequence recorded explicitly: **a deployment whose owner has not yet created a Branch cannot approve a registration** — correctly, because there is nowhere to place the applicant | SRS §0, §4.4b, §15.1, TD-4.6b + new TD-4.6d; TASKS M3b | None yet |
| 2026-08-04 | SRS-R43.2 | **Implementation correction found by writing the migration — the specified constraint was not expressible.** Revision 43 stated the *at most one Teaching Group per (student, Subject, Level)* rule as **"a functional unique index over the join"**. **A PostgreSQL unique index cannot reference another table**, so that sentence described something that cannot be built; the defect surfaced on the first attempt to write the SQL, not later in a code review. `StudentTeachingGroup` now carries `subject_id` and `level_id` **on the row**, constrained by a composite FK to `TeachingGroup(id, subject_id, level_id)` — the identical technique `Enrollment` already uses for the identical reason, so the schema has **one idea applied twice rather than two ideas**. `TeachingGroup` gains the redundant `UNIQUE (id, subject_id, level_id)` that FK needs as a target, exactly as `AdministrativeGroup` carries `UNIQUE (id, level_id)`. **The rule is unchanged; only its mechanism is** — recorded as a numbered revision rather than silently substituted, because a reader comparing the schema against R43's wording would otherwise find a discrepancy with no explanation | SRS §7, TD-4.6a, TD-6 | `20260804101600_r43_educational_model_constraints` |
| 2026-08-04 | M3b-1 | **Revision 43 expand-phase schema and migrations, with every constraint PROVEN to reject the row it exists to reject.** Two migrations (TD-6b expand phase): `_r43_educational_model_expand` adds 8 tables — `AdministrativeGroup`, `TeachingGroup`, `StudentTeachingGroup`, `Enrollment`, `RecurringCourseSchedule`, `CourseScheduleStaff`, `Session`, `SessionContent`, `EventAdministrativeGroup` — plus 3 enums, 2 new `recurrence_type` values, `Room.capacity`, and `Grade.administrative_group_id`. **Purely additive:** `group`, `group_teacher`, `student_group`, `event_group` and `EducationalContent.event_id` are untouched and still read by released code; the contract migration that drops them is a separate, later one. `_r43_educational_model_constraints` hand-writes what Prisma cannot express (TD-6a): both composite FKs, two partial unique indexes, the mode/target CHECK, the recurrence-shape CHECK, time-order CHECKs, `ar-x-icu` collations on both new `name` columns. **Verification is the point of this entry.** Both migrations were applied to a scratch database **from empty**, then an 18-case proof script (`backend/prisma/verification/r43-constraints-proof.sql`, committed) attempted every row each constraint exists to refuse: **13 rejections fired, each on the named constraint, and the 5 legitimate rows were accepted.** Proven directly in SQL rather than through a service — the composite FK rejects an `Enrollment` whose `level_id` disagrees with its group's, and rejects a teaching-group seat that lies about its subject. **Case 5 is the proof of independence between Subjects** the Document Owner asked for: a Tajweed seat for a student who already holds a Quran seat *in the same Level* is accepted, while a second Quran seat is refused. **One defect in my own constraint was caught by this run and would not have been caught any other way:** the cancellation-reason CHECK was first written as `(status <> 'cancelled' AND reason IS NULL) OR (status = 'cancelled' AND btrim(reason) <> '')`, which **silently accepts a NULL reason** — `btrim(NULL) <> ''` is `NULL`, not `false`, and a CHECK treats `NULL` as satisfied. The first proof run showed 13 errors but no cancellation error, and the cancelled row had quietly taken the `(schedule, date)` slot, which is how it was noticed. Rewritten as `status <> 'cancelled' OR (reason IS NOT NULL AND btrim(reason) <> '')`, re-proven. The reasoning is now a comment above the constraint so nobody rewrites it into the elegant-looking broken form | `schema.prisma` +9 models/enums with the rationale inline; proof script committed under `prisma/verification/` | `20260804101500_r43_educational_model_expand`, `20260804101600_r43_educational_model_constraints` |
| 2026-08-04 | M3b-2 | **Roster resolution and teacher scope — the single definition §4.4c mandates, with both claims mutation-proven.** `policies/roster-resolution.ts` is the one place that answers *"which students is this for"*, and every other rule cites it rather than restating it: the consent gate (BR-2), attendance when it ships (§4.7), teacher scope (TD-2), and every roster query. **Composable `where` fragments, not id lists** — the reasoning `teacher-scope.ts` already records: materialising ids takes a snapshot, and between resolving the set and using it a student can be enrolled or moved, so the request would act on a roster that is no longer true. **The audience is never stored** (§20 rule 22); it resolves at read time from the schedule's mode and target. **Teacher scope replaces `GroupTeacher`** with `CourseScheduleStaff`, and `schedule.branch_id` now states a teacher's branch scope **directly** where the old path inferred it through two hops. **One honest compromise, documented at the call site:** the administrative-group and teaching-group arms are fully relational (they reach the staff table through the target's own `schedules` back-relation and never leave the database), but the entire-level arm needs `enrollment.administrativeGroup.branchId` to equal `schedule.branchId`, and **Prisma cannot correlate two sibling relations in one `where`** — so those schedules' `(levelId, branchId)` pairs are read first. The *student* side stays live in all three arms; only the teacher's own staffing is read at an instant, and it is re-read next call. The alternative — dropping the branch bound to keep it in one query — would silently widen a teacher's reach to students at other branches, which is a correctness failure, not a performance one. **17 integration tests against the Document Owner's own example** (Level 1: Quran split 2 ways, Tajweed 1 way, Tafsir whole-level; هدى in AdminGroup A + Quran-1 + Tajweed-1 simultaneously). **Both load-bearing claims proven by reintroducing the bug:** removing the entire-level branch bound fails 2 tests; resolving a subject split to the administrative roster instead — the exact conflation R43 removed — fails 4. Also pinned: assistants have identical reach to teachers (one table, one rule), a teacher with no schedules reaches nobody, revoking a staffing ends reach on the next call, and a mode/target disagreement **throws rather than resolving a wrong roster** — the database refuses such a row, so reaching that branch means the schema was bypassed and failing loudly beats resolving something plausible | `roster-resolution.ts` + `.integration.test.ts`; no second implementation of any arm | None — reads only |
| 2026-08-04 | FIX-P0-HIJRI | **`/superadmin/hijri-calendar` rendered blank white — the adapter's declared types were fiction, and nothing on either side of the contract could see it.** **Root cause:** `adapters/hijri-calendar.ts` named `hijri_year` / `months` / `hijri_month_ar` against the API's real `year` / `data` / `month_name_ar`. The page did `data?.months.filter(…)` — the `?.` guarded `data` being null but **not `months` being `undefined`** — so `.filter()` threw a TypeError during render, React unmounted the tree, and the screen went blank with **no error visible anywhere**. **Why nothing caught it, which is the part worth keeping:** `api<T>()` is an **unchecked cast** — the generic *asserts* a shape and nothing parses one — so the wrong type compiled, passed every frontend test that built its fixtures from that same wrong type, and was invisible to `curl` because the server's bytes were always correct. It could fail only in a browser. **Two guards, on opposite sides, because neither catches the other's drift.** *Server:* the HTTP suite now pins the **exact key set** of the envelope and of a month row; `toMatchObject` could never have caught this, since it checks a subset and is blind to a *missing* field. *Client:* `pages/admin/hijri-calendar.test.tsx` declares a `WIRE` fixture **typed as the adapter's own interface** with the six keys the server test pins — so renaming a field breaks the **typecheck**, which is precisely the check the cast cannot perform. **Proven by reintroducing the exact original bug: reverting the adapter to `hijri_year`/`months`/`hijri_month_ar` now fails the typecheck in 8 places, where before it compiled silently.** **Also fixed:** `HijriMonthRecorded` split out as its own type, because the write response carries `hijri_year` and omits `month_name_ar` — one type across two shapes is what let the mismatch hide. The three inline derivations moved into an exported, tested `summariseYear()`; three expressions inside a component body cannot be tested, and one of them was the crash. **Confirmed NOT a defect:** the public calendar's `hijri_month_ar` is a *different, correct* contract (`calendar.controller.ts`), so the two names legitimately coexist — the four remaining occurrences in the bundle all belong to it. **Verified live:** shell serves `Cache-Control: no-cache`, `index.html` points at the rebuilt bundle, the served JS carries `month_name_ar`, and the admin route answers `401 AUTH_REQUIRED` in the TD-3.8 envelope. 31 backend Hijri tests · 219 frontend tests (11 new) · build clean | `frontend.md` gains *"`api<T>()` is an unchecked cast"* with the two-guard table; `testing.md` gains the client half of the key-set rule | None |
| 2026-08-04 | SRS-R43.3 | **A contradiction inside Revision 43 itself, found before writing the Teaching Group service.** TD-2 granted Admins `✔ (own branches)` over *"Manage Teaching Groups and their membership"*, while §7/§4.4c define a Teaching Group as carrying **only** `subject_id` and `level_id` — and §4.4b makes a Level explicitly branch-independent. **"Own branches" therefore had no referent**, and the scope check every other operational service performs could not be written at all. The concrete failure it permitted: a Marrakesh Admin creating, renaming or **deleting** the Quran split for a Level whose students also sit at Targa, while the unassigned list showed them only Marrakesh students — **authority over everyone, visibility of some**. **Resolution (Document Owner decision): the TD-2 row splits in two.** **Teaching Group CRUD is Super Admin only**, joining the Levels and Subjects it organises under Revision 26's rule that curriculum *structure* is reference data. **Membership stays an Admin action**, scoped by the branch the student is actually enrolled at (`Enrollment → AdministrativeGroup.branch_id`) — a referent that exists. **Rejected:** deriving a scope from *"this Admin runs a group in this Level"* (it leaves two Admins jointly authoritative over one Level's splits, which is the hazard rather than a fix), and adding `branch_id` to `TeachingGroup` (the most domain-accurate answer, since students do not commute between branches — recorded as the option to revisit, and deliberately not taken while no Teaching Group rows exist to migrate) | SRS §0, §4.4c, §5.6, TD-2 | None |
| 2026-08-04 | M3b-3 | **The educational-organisation services: TD-4.6b, TD-4.6d, enrolment, Teaching Groups, and the unassigned list.** Five services, no endpoints — the HTTP layer is a later M3b task. **TD-4.6b `createLevel`** writes the Level and **المجموعة 1 at the supplied Branch in one transaction**, so *"a Level with no group"* never exists rather than existing until something fills it in; the Branch is an **input, not a column on `Level`** (adding one would make a Level branch-local and break `entire_level` teaching mode). **TD-4.6d `backfillFirstGroups`** runs **inside** `createBranch`, keyed on *"every Level with no live group"* rather than on *"is this the first branch"* — which makes it naturally idempotent, a no-op on every later branch, and correct even if the first branch is later soft-deleted. One audit row for the whole backfill, not one per group: it was a single operator action with a single cause. **Enrolment** reads `level_id` **from the group, never from the caller**, so the composite FK is a backstop rather than the primary defence; **there is no capacity check anywhere** (BR-23), and the comment says why removing the lock with it was safe. `moveStudent` re-points the row in place rather than deleting and re-creating, so `enrolled_at` survives and the student is never momentarily in no group. **Teaching Groups** implement the R43.3 split; the **unassigned list** returns `split: false` with an empty list when a Subject is not split at all — deliberately distinguishable from *"everyone is assigned"*, because returning every enrolled student there would read as an alarm. **43 integration tests, organised by the rule they defend rather than the function they call. Three mutations prove the load-bearing ones:** removing the first-group creation from TD-4.6b fails **31** tests; checking BR-21 per-group instead of per-Level fails the one test that names it; and making Teaching Group uniqueness per-Level instead of per-(Subject, Level) — which is exactly what would destroy independence between subjects — fails *"one student sits in two different subjects' splits at once"* | `level.service.ts`, `administrative-group.service.ts`, `enrollment.service.ts`, `teaching-group.service.ts` added; `branch.service.ts` gains the TD-4.6d call | None — uses the R43 expand-phase tables |
