| 2026-08-05 | M3b-16 | **The Moroccan Hijri constraint re-examined; automation rejected again, and the real failure mode fixed instead.** The Document Owner restated *the system MUST always follow the official Moroccan Hijri calendar* as a project constraint and asked whether something more robust than manual maintenance was possible. **Re-examined from scratch, and the constraint is precisely what rules automation out.** Every automatable Hijri calendar is a *calculation* and Morocco does not calculate: **Umm al-Qura** is Saudi Arabia's calculated calendar and diverges by design; **tabular/arithmetic** uses a fixed 30-year leap cycle and cannot represent an observation-based calendar at all; **astronomical conjunction and visibility models** predict when the crescent *could* be seen, while Morocco declares when it *was* seen by naked eye from Moroccan territory — the two disagree whenever weather or judgement intervenes; and **scraping the Ministry** means parsing prose news posts with no stable structure, published *after* the sighting, where a parser fails silently and yields a wrong date. **An automated overlay would not be occasionally imprecise, it would be confidently wrong — and a wrong official date is a worse failure than a missing one.** Automation does not serve this constraint, it violates it; **Revision 32 stands unchanged and no importer ships.** **What WAS worth fixing is operational, not correctness.** Silence over guessing was already right — `baseHijri` returns `null` for an unannounced month and dates render Gregorian-only. But when the recorded months run out, that happens with **no error, no log and no screen state**: a manually maintained dataset degrading in silence is how a feature stops working without anyone noticing. So `GET /admin/hijri-calendar` now carries a **`coverage`** block — `published_through`, `days_remaining`, `warning`, `next_unrecorded` — on the screen that exists to maintain it. **Four decisions inside it, each load-bearing:** it **computes no Hijri date** (arithmetic on Gregorian dates the Ministry supplied, so the constraint is untouched); `days_remaining` counts to the **29-day floor, not 30**, because day 30 resolves only when the next *consecutive* month is recorded and counting to 30 would promise runway the resolver will not deliver; it **goes negative rather than clamping**, since *expired 40 days ago* and *expires today* call for different urgency; and it is **`null`, never `0`, when nothing is published**, because *nobody has recorded anything* and *it ran out today* are different answers and a screen showing `0` for both reports an expiry that never existed. **Only published months count** (§5.7 renders only those; a draft is runway the platform will not use). **No route was added** (§20 rule 16) — this extends the response of the endpoint that already exists for exactly this job. **The existing HTTP test caught the addition**, which is what pinning an exact wire shape is for; it was updated to pin the new block too. **Analysis recorded in `architecture/calendar-and-hijri.md`** with the rejected candidates in a table, so the next person to ask *why not just use a library* finds the answer rather than re-deriving it. 4 new service tests; 12 HTTP tests still green. |
